.TH CCOL_CREATE_CHTTPSVR_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp \- create an HTTP server with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                            clog cl,
                            char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a new chttpsvr handle and initializes it.  It uses the
memory management procedures that you give.  It also creates a root
router with an empty prefix automatically.

mprocs may be NULL.  The function then uses the default system
allocator.

cl controls the logger of the server, and it may be CLOG_INVALID:

- cl == CLOG_INVALID: the server creates its own internal logger.
  That logger writes only FATAL messages to stderr.
- cl != CLOG_INVALID: the server derives a new logger from cl.  See
  clog_derive.  It tags that logger with the field
  component=http-server.  The server uses and manages the derived
  logger.  The cl handle of the caller does not change, and the caller
  keeps ownership of it.

In both cases the server closes its own logger automatically when
something destroys the server.  This is true for the internal logger
and for the derived one.

On a failure the function sets err_str to a static diagnostic string,
when err_str is not NULL, and returns CHTTPSVR_INVALID.

chttpsvr is an opaque value handle (a uint64_t), not a pointer.
Before the library uses the server, it resolves the handle through a
table that it owns, and it checks a generation.  Do not cast the handle
to or from a pointer.  Compare it with CHTTPSVR_INVALID, which is 0.  A
truth test such as if (!srv) therefore also works.  The library refuses a
call with the handle of a destroyed server.  Where chttpsvr_destroy(3)
says so, such a call is fatal.  Such a call never causes a
use-after-free.

There are two supported ways to run more than one process that serves
traffic with this library.  The first is a fork(2) before the process
creates and starts any server.  Each process that results, the parent
and the child alike, then calls ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp and
chttpsvr_start on its own, and builds its own server from nothing.
The second is a fork(2) with an exec(3) immediately after it.  That is
the pattern behind popen, behind system, and behind the launch of a
subprocess from inside a request handler.  It is fully supported
whether or not a server runs at that moment.  The program that exec(3)
starts replaces the process image completely, and never touches
anything that this library left behind.  A process that continues to
run with no exec(3) in between, while a server that it created before
the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.

The library adds one defense-in-depth measure on top of that.  A fresh
call to ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp, chttpsvr_start, chttpsvr_stop,
chttpsvr_destroy, chttpsvr_register_handler,
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler, chttpsvr_use or
chttpsvr_subrouter never hangs on a lock.  This holds for a call from
any thread, in the parent and in a newly forked child with no exec(3)
after it.  Such a lock is one that some other thread held at the
instant of the fork, and that thread may no longer exist, because
fork(2) duplicates only the calling thread.  This measure also covers
a fork that lands in the middle of a start, a stop or a destroy of
that exact handle.  It covers a fork in the middle of a shutdown of
the whole shared engine that chttpsvr_engine_stop() drives.

A server that is live and actively runs work at the moment of such a
fork is a different matter, and this measure does not change it.  Such
a server is explicitly out of scope.  Its reactor threads, its
worker-pool threads and its idle-timeout-sweep threads do not go into
the child.  A server that the child inherits, and that was already
started, can therefore no longer accept new connections there, and it
can no longer process in-flight work there.  A call to
chttpsvr_stop() and then chttpsvr_start() on that exact handle in the
child does not bring it back either.  That call reports ccol_success,
but the server still cannot serve.  The server reuses the engine
reference that it already held before the fork, and does not build a
new one.  A caller that needs none of this protection can compile it
out with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A valid chttpsvr handle on success, or CHTTPSVR_INVALID on a failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpsvr (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR clog_derive (3)
